bcc-cc1: string concatenation across the end of the input buffer.

bcc-cc1 joins adjacent string constants on one line ("a" "b"), looking past
the space after the first with *++lineptr. When that space ran up to the end
of the block it had read from bcc-cpp, it found the buffer's end mark
instead of the next string, and the two strings were left apart: a "need
')'" error on a correct line, which came and went with the length of the
file's name (bcc-cpp's # line marks put it in the output, moving the block
boundaries). gch1() reads the next character the way the scanner does,
refilling the buffer at its end.

--- a/bcc/scan.c
+++ b/bcc/scan.c
@@ -868,8 +868,10 @@
 	{
 	    *charptr++ = terminator;
 
-	    /* This adds ansi string concatenation BUT only on one line */
-	    do { ch = *++lineptr; } while(SYMOFCHAR(ch) == WHITESPACE);
+	    /* This adds ansi string concatenation BUT only on one line.
+	     * gch1(), not *++lineptr: the space between the strings can
+	     * run past the end of the input buffer, which gch1 refills */
+	    do { gch1(); } while(SYMOFCHAR(ch) == WHITESPACE);
 	    if( ch == terminator ) /* Hang on .. that's another string ... */
 	    {
 	       charptr--;	   /* Stick it together */
